    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8aab3061_b3c3f0d1",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/SocketKeepalive.java",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 178,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-01-21T03:54:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This at least needs comments : I don\u0027t see any case where it\u0027s legitimate to call this (though the implementation is doing it in many places). This simply stops message processing but does not stop the actual keepalive right ?\n\nThat being said, KA02.5 completely removes this method, so if you plan to check it in, you can ignore this comment (or possibly just leave a TODO to remove the method).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 178,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 178,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "1cf4d35d8db693812b21afb9559063b99b7249bf",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e1fd87ff_760f2b37",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/SocketKeepalive.java",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 186,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-01-21T03:54:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Wait final ? So implementors *must* override start() but *must not* override this ?\nThis sounds like asking for trouble.\n\nI understand you\u0027re concerned about apps overriding this as exemplified by the comments on patchset 9. I think you should have a way to do this internally only, for example :\n\npublic final void start(int intervalSec) {\n  startImpl(intervalSec);\n}\n\nabstract void startImpl(int intervalSec);\n\n\n...and the same for stop(). This way only internal implementations can override but they can override both.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 186,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 186,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "1cf4d35d8db693812b21afb9559063b99b7249bf",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e800f6ce_775446e3",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/SocketKeepalive.java",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 188,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-01-21T03:54:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "(Not sure how my comment relates to Lorenzo\u0027s on patchset 9. Maybe Lorenzo was hinting at the same thing ? Junyu, does your reply there address my concern here ? I have trouble seeing how your suggestion about restarting will fix that, but if it will please feel free to tell me to go pound sand).\n\nThis is public API so this can be called on any thread.\n\nSo the client calls startKeepalive. The system starts it and sends a message to the handler in l.129. The control reaches l.140 but the thread is pre-empted after the test and before the assignment.\nMeanwhile the client calls stop(). mSlot is null and this method misfires. The handler thread is resumed and keepalive goes on.\nNote that it does not look to me like KA02.5 will fix this.\n\nGenerally speaking I don\u0027t actually understand why you made mSlot volatile : it doesn\u0027t buy you anything considering the code. I suspect pushing a message to the thread to stop keepalive may be the easiest way to keep this race-free and lock-free.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 188,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 188,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "1cf4d35d8db693812b21afb9559063b99b7249bf",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "df12223d_5c04e480",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/SocketKeepalive.java",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 189,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-01-21T03:54:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "General concern (food for thought, not necessarily needs some change) : from a software engineering point of view is it fine to have abstract start() but concrete stop() that calls mService.stopKeepalive ? It sounds to me like strong coupling and requiring the subclasses to call some form of starting the keepalive ; at the same time it means the children classes are entitled to have a start() method but no corresponding stop() method which makes them harder to understand. It may make sense as a default implementation, but then it raises the question of whether start() should have a default implementation too.\n\nJust food for thought.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 189,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 189,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "1cf4d35d8db693812b21afb9559063b99b7249bf",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
